module postgres::pool;

//! Several connections, and a fair way to take one.
//!
//! `db.kh` gives one connection a serving fiber and a `Db` over it. That is
//! correct and it is not concurrent: a second fiber's statements queue behind
//! the first's, however many workers are free.
//!
//! A pool is the obvious fix and, given a channel, barely a thing at all.
//! **The pool is a channel holding the idle connections.** Taking one is a
//! receive, giving it back is a send, and waiting when they are all busy is
//! what a channel already does. There is no free list, no condition variable
//! and no count of who has what -- those are the parts a pool usually is, and
//! they are all the channel's.
//!
//! # Backpressure comes for free, and that is the point
//!
//! A pool of four with five fibers wanting a connection does not queue the
//! fifth request against the database and it does not fail it. The fifth
//! *fiber* waits, having given its worker back, until somebody finishes. Under
//! more load than the pool can serve, the waiting happens at the edge -- which
//! is what stops a service from accepting work until it runs out of memory.
//!
//! # Leases, capabilities, and the region they belong to
//!
//! `with_db` takes a connection, installs it as `db: Db` for the body, and must
//! give it back including when the body raises and including when the fiber
//! running it is cancelled. Application code therefore keeps `Db` in its
//! capability row instead of accepting a connection value just because the
//! provider happens to be a pool.
//!
//! Returning the lease is a region's job: the return is registered with
//! `Region::defer` before the body runs, and a region's finalizers run on the
//! way out however the way out is reached.
//!
//! **Before the body is not early enough; it has to be before the first
//! cancellation point after the receive.** The first version registered it
//! through `scoped` and `acquire`, each of which checks for a cancellation
//! when it is entered. A fiber cancelled while it waited for a connection,
//! and handed one at the same moment, stopped at that check holding a
//! connection that no finalizer knew about. A pool of one lost its only
//! connection within two hundred such cancels, and then `with_db` and `close`
//! both waited for ever. `Region::open` and `Region::defer` are not
//! cancellation points, so nothing lies between the receive and the
//! registration.
//!
//! **The region has to be `with_db`'s own**, and the first version got this
//! wrong in a way worth recording. It required a `Scope` from its caller,
//! which reads as flexible and means the lease is held until the *caller's*
//! region ends rather than until the call returns. Four jobs against a pool of
//! two, all inside one `scoped`, took both connections and gave neither back:
//! the third waited for ever. A pool of `n` behaved like a pool of `n` uses.
//!
//! So `with_db` opens a region per lease. A caller needs no `Scope` capability
//! for it, and the `Db` requirement disappears at this boundary because the
//! leased handler is installed here.
//!
//! **The transaction is covered too**, as of 13.3: `std::db::transaction`
//! registers its rollback with a region of its own, so a fiber stopped
//! mid-flight sends the `ROLLBACK` on its way out rather than leaving it to
//! the server to notice when the connection is reused. The lease then comes
//! back to a connection that is ready, which is why the two belong together.
//!
//! # Reconnecting, shrinking, and growing back
//!
//! **Each slot's serving fiber owns its connection's whole life**, including
//! losing it. `with_db` learns how a connection is doing only by asking the
//! fiber that owns it.
//!
//! - A slot is in `idle` only while it is connected. `open` puts no slot
//!   there; each serving fiber puts its own there once its connection opens.
//! - Every lease starts with a `Check`: is the socket still open (a server
//!   restart closes idle connections, and nothing reads an idle socket to
//!   notice), and is the connection outside a transaction? A slot that fails
//!   is not lent: its serving fiber marks it broken, `with_db` gives it back
//!   at once and takes the next one.
//! - Every lease ends by closing the request channel the slot offered for
//!   it; the serving fiber answers what is still queued, and the close is
//!   how it learns the lease is over. That is the only way back into `idle`,
//!   so a slot that broke during the lease (a cut-off reply, `broken`, a
//!   `ROLLBACK` an `abort` cut short) is reconnected instead of lent again.
//!   A close needs no room and cannot be refused, so no way of stopping the
//!   borrower -- and no number of fibers it left with requests queued --
//!   keeps the lease from ending.
//! - Reconnecting closes the old socket first and never reads it again, then
//!   tries on the pool's fast `Schedule`. Meanwhile the slot counts as
//!   `reconnecting`, and callers wait for it.
//! - When the fast schedule gives up, the slot **shrinks out**: it counts as
//!   `down`, and tries again on the slow interval until it connects (and
//!   rejoins `idle`) or the pool closes.
//! - **A pool with no slot live or reconnecting fails its callers at once**,
//!   with the reason the last attempt failed. The callers already waiting
//!   are woken by the down token (`Offer::Down`), put into `idle` when the
//!   last slot shrinks. A caller that receives it puts it back for the next
//!   one and answers `Disconnected`; once a slot is live or reconnecting
//!   again, the next caller to receive it drops it.
//!
//! What it costs: a request channel per lease, one `Check` message per lease,
//! and one non-blocking read. What it does not see: a
//! connection that dies between the `Check` and the borrower's first
//! statement. That statement is answered `Disconnected`, and the slot
//! reconnects when the lease ends.

import std::core::{Changed, Channel, Fiber, Fibers, Option, Region, Result, Shared};
import std::clock::{Clock};
import std::db::{Db, DbError};
import std::random::{Random};
import std::resilience::{Schedule};
import postgres::conn::{Connection, usable};
import postgres::db::{Fitness, Request, Settings, connect, fitness, hang_up, over, serve_lease};

/// A set of connections, each with a fiber serving it.
pub type Pool = {
  /// What is ready to lend: a slot whose connection is open and unused, as
  /// the request channel for its next lease -- plus, at most once, the down
  /// token. The module doc has both.
  idle: Channel<Offer>,
  /// The fibers doing the serving.
  ///
  /// **Held here because a pool that did not hold them would stop working on
  /// its way out of `open`.** A caller with no later use for its nursery hands
  /// over its last reference, so the nursery is released when `open` returns —
  /// and releasing a nursery cancels its children and waits for them, which is
  /// every serving fiber this just started. The symptom is a pool that hangs
  /// before it is ever used, and the cause is two rooms away.
  ///
  /// Keeping it is also the honest ownership: these fibers exist for this pool
  /// and end with it, which is what `close` then has something to wait for.
  crew: Fibers,
  /// How many slots are in each state, the last reason a connection failed,
  /// and whether the pool is closing.
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
};

/// How a pool reconnects a connection it has lost.
pub type Reconnect = {
  /// The fast phase: when to try again after a failure, and when to give up.
  /// A slot whose schedule gives up shrinks out of the pool.
  fast: Schedule,
  /// The slow phase: how often, in milliseconds, a slot that shrank out
  /// tries again, with 80-120% jitter. `None` makes the shrink permanent.
  slow: Option<Int>,
  /// How long, in milliseconds, a connection attempt waits for the server to
  /// finish its handshake before it counts as failed and the schedule goes
  /// on. Without it, a server that accepts and never answers held the slot,
  /// its waiting callers, and `close` for ever. The TCP connect before the
  /// handshake is not covered: see "What it cannot cut short" on `close`.
  handshake: Int,
};

impl Reconnect {
  /// Exponential backoff from 50 ms, doubling to a 5 s cap, with 50-100%
  /// jitter, for at most 30 s; then a try every 30 s. A handshake gets 10 s.
  ///
  /// **Why these numbers.** The fast phase is for the ordinary bad minute --
  /// a server restart, a failover, a dropped route -- and callers wait
  /// through it, so its total is a caller's patience. 30 s is HikariCP's
  /// default `connectionTimeout`, how long a borrower there waits before it
  /// is failed. The backoff is HikariCP's own shape for creating connections
  /// (10 ms doubling to a 5 s cap); 50 ms first still reaches a server that
  /// was only restarting on the first or second try, and the 5 s cap keeps a
  /// pool of 20 from making 20 connection attempts a second at a server that
  /// is down. The jitter spreads a fleet's retries apart after an outage
  /// they all saw at once.
  ///
  /// The slow phase is why a pool shrunk to nothing grows back without a
  /// restart. HikariCP keeps creating connections up to its minimum, which is
  /// the pool size by default, and its housekeeper runs every 30 s; 30 s is
  /// that. Other pools do less: pgx refills in the background only up to
  /// `MinConns`, which is zero unless set, and deadpool and node-postgres
  /// create a connection only when a caller asks, so their callers meet the
  /// connect error themselves. A slot here costs one attempt per 30 s
  /// against a server that is down.
  ///
  /// 10 s for a handshake is pgJDBC's default `connectTimeout`, and ten times
  /// what a healthy server needs even over a slow link. libpq's
  /// `connect_timeout` and pgx's `ConnectTimeout` default to no bound at all,
  /// which is the hang this field exists to rule out.
  pub fn default() -> Reconnect {
    {
      fast: Schedule::UpTo(Schedule::backoff(50, 5000), 30000), slow: Option::Some(30000),
      handshake: 10000,
    }
  }

  /// One attempt, then shrink for good.
  pub fn never() -> Reconnect {
    { fast: Schedule::once(), slow: Option::None, handshake: 10000 }
  }
}

/// What `with_db` takes from `idle`.
///
/// **The down token is a case of its own, not a channel that looks like
/// one.** Told apart by the shape of a channel -- one with a request already
/// queued -- a slot could pass for the token whenever something sent to it
/// outside a lease, a detached fiber still holding an old lease's `db` for
/// one, and its give-back then kept the slot out of `idle` for good.
pub type Offer =
  /// A slot, as the request channel for this one lease. Its serving fiber
  /// makes a fresh one for every lease and reads it until it is closed.
  | Slot(Channel<Request>)
  /// There is nothing to lend: no slot is live or reconnecting.
  | Down;

/// How many slots are in each state. See `health`.
pub type Health = {
  /// Connected: idle, or lent to somebody.
  live: Int,
  /// Lost, and being reconnected on the fast schedule. Callers wait for
  /// these.
  reconnecting: Int,
  /// Shrunk out: the fast schedule gave up. Retrying on the slow interval,
  /// if there is one, and never lent until a retry succeeds.
  down: Int,
};

/// The pool's shared bookkeeping, changed only under its lock.
type Tally = {
  live: Int,
  reconnecting: Int,
  down: Int,
  /// Why the last connection attempt failed.
  last: String,
  closing: Bool,
  /// Whether the down token is in `idle`, so there is never more than one.
  token: Bool,
};

/// Where a slot is.
type Phase =
  | Live
  | Reconnecting
  | Down;

/// One try at a lease.
type Tried<A> =
  | Done(A)
  /// The slot failed its `Check` and has been given back; take another.
  | Again
  | Refused(DbError);

/// Opens a pool of `count` connections that reconnects with
/// `Reconnect::default()`.
pub fn open(crew: Fibers, settings: Settings, count: Int) -> Pool {
  open_with(crew, settings, count, Reconnect::default())
}

/// Opens `count` slots and adopts a serving fiber for each into `crew`.
///
/// **Returns before any of them has connected.** A serving fiber opens its own
/// connection, so this is a spawn and not a round trip. `with_db` waits for
/// the first connection to open; a pool that cannot connect at all answers
/// `Disconnected` with the reason once every slot's fast schedule has given
/// up.
///
/// Below one connection is one.
pub fn open_with(crew: Fibers, settings: Settings, count: Int, plan: Reconnect) -> Pool {
  let wanted = if count < 1 { 1 } else { count };
  // One more than the slots, for the down token. Each slot has at most one
  // offer here at a time: it makes the next only once the last lease is over.
  let idle: Channel<Offer> = Channel::bounded(wanted + 1);
  let tally = Shared::of({
      live: 0, reconnecting: wanted, down: 0, last: "no connection has been tried yet",
      closing: false, token: false,
    });
  let mut made = 0;
  while made < wanted {
    Fibers::adopt(crew, Fiber::spawn(fn () => slot(settings, plan, idle, tally)));
    made = made + 1
  };
  { idle: idle, crew: crew, tally: tally }
}

/// How many slots are live, reconnecting, and shrunk out, right now.
///
/// For tests and health checks. A pool with none live or reconnecting answers
/// every `with_db` with `Disconnected` at once.
pub fn health(pool: Pool) -> Health {
  let t = Shared::get(pool.tally);
  { live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down }
}

/// Runs `body` with a leased connection installed as the `db: Db` capability.
///
/// Waits while every connection is busy or reconnecting. The connection goes
/// back **when this call returns**, however it returns -- normally, raising,
/// or cancelled -- because the lease is registered with a region this
/// function opens, before anything after the receive can be cancelled.
///
/// **Answers `Err(Disconnected(reason))` at once when no connection is live
/// or reconnecting**, with the reason the last attempt failed, rather than
/// waiting for a slow retry that may be half a minute away.
///
/// The `db` requirement is handled here and removed from the caller's row. Any
/// other requirements and raised failures on `body` pass straight through.
/// The `Result` is only about obtaining the lease.
pub fn with_db<A, 'ef, 'er>(
  pool: Pool,
  body: () -> A with { 'ef | db: Db } raises 'er,
) -> Result<A, DbError>
  with 'ef
  raises 'er
{
  // **A loop, not a call to itself.** A slot whose server finishes the
  // handshake and then fails every check offers a new connection every few
  // milliseconds; taking each by a call to `with_db` cost a frame apiece,
  // and a caller that waited through a few seconds of that ran out of stack.
  let mut tried = one_try(pool, body)!;
  while again(tried) { tried = one_try(pool, body)! };
  match tried {
    Tried::Done(answer) => Result::Ok(answer),
    // Not reached: the loop leaves only on `Done` or `Refused`.
    Tried::Again => Result::Err(DbError::Disconnected("the pool is closed")),
    Tried::Refused(why) => Result::Err(why),
  }
}

fn again<A>(tried: Tried<A>) -> Bool {
  match tried { Tried::Again => true, Tried::Done(_) => false, Tried::Refused(_) => false }
}

/// One offer from `idle` and its lease, which ends when this returns.
fn one_try<A, 'ef, 'er>(
  pool: Pool,
  body: () -> A with { 'ef | db: Db } raises 'er,
) -> Tried<A>
  with 'ef
  raises 'er
{
  match Channel::receive(pool.idle) {
    Option::None => Tried::Refused(DbError::Disconnected("the pool is closed")),
    // **The give-back is registered before anything in this function can
    // poll.** A receive that returns a value has handed the connection to
    // this fiber, and from then on only a registered finalizer can bring it
    // back. `Region::open` and `Region::defer` are not cancellation points;
    // `scoped`, `acquire` and `Scope::defer` are all entered through a poll,
    // and a cancel taken at any of them unwound holding a connection nobody
    // else knew about. One such cancel costs the pool a connection, for good,
    // and when the last one goes every `with_db` and `close` waits for ever.
    //
    // The region is this arm's, so the lease ends when the arm does: before
    // a slot that failed its `Check` is replaced by another, not after.
    Option::Some(offer) => {
      let lease = Region::open();
      Region::defer(lease, returner(pool.idle, pool.tally, offer));
      match offer {
        Offer::Slot(requests) => match fitness(requests) {
          Fitness::Fit => Tried::Done(body()! with { db: over(requests) }),
          Fitness::Unfit => Tried::Again,
          // A lease's channel is closed only by its own give-back, and by
          // `close` while it sits in `idle`; neither can have happened yet.
          // Trying again meets the closed `idle` if the pool is closing.
          Fitness::Gone => Tried::Again,
        },
        Offer::Down => answer_down(pool),
      }
    },
  }
}

/// A caller was handed the down token: the reason, or another try if a
/// connection has come back since the token was made.
///
/// **Only decides; the lease's give-back does the passing on.** Sending the
/// token back to `idle` from here would come after several cancellation
/// points, and a caller stopped before the send would unwind holding it:
/// `tally` would still say a token was out, so no other would ever be made,
/// and every later `with_db` would wait for ever. The give-back re-offers it
/// under the rule this leaves in `tally.token`, and it runs however the
/// caller stops.
fn answer_down<A>(pool: Pool) -> Tried<A> {
  let keep = Shared::modify(pool.tally, fn t => {
      let down = !t.closing && t.live + t.reconnecting == 0;
      {
        state: { live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: t.last,
          closing: t.closing, token: down },
        result: down,
      }
    });
  if keep { Tried::Refused(DbError::Disconnected(Shared::get(pool.tally).last)) } else { Tried::Again }
}

/// The give-back `with_db` registers, built outside `with_db`.
///
/// **What this prevents: an `abort` losing the lease without running a line
/// of the give-back.** A lambda written inside a function that calls a
/// function value polls when it is entered, because it may be the far end of
/// that call. `with_db` calls `body`, so a give-back written inline there
/// polled first, and an `abort` -- which stops cleanup at its next
/// cancellation point -- landing while a transaction's `ROLLBACK` waited
/// stopped it at that poll. The give-back never ran, the pool was one
/// connection short for good, and at none every later `with_db` and `close`
/// waited for ever. Built here, in a function that calls no function value,
/// the lambda has no entry poll.
///
/// **Whether the lease is the down token is fixed here, from the offer's
/// case, and never learned by trying a send.** A send that fails is followed
/// by a cancellation check, and an aborted caller stops at it, so a give-back
/// that branched on a send's answer never got as far as acting on it.
fn returner(idle: Channel<Offer>, tally: Shared<Tally>, offer: Offer) -> () -> () {
  fn () => give_back(idle, tally, offer)
}

/// Ends a lease, in one step that cannot wait and cannot fail.
///
/// **A slot's lease ends by closing its request channel.** The serving
/// fiber answers whatever is still queued, reads the close as the end of the
/// lease, and decides whether to lend the connection again or reconnect it.
///
/// A message on that channel would need room, and that is how leases were
/// lost: a body whose own fibers each had a request queued could leave the
/// channel full, an aborted fiber does not wait for room, and the dropped
/// message left the serving fiber waiting for ever on a lease that had ended.
/// No capacity is enough, because the number of fibers is the caller's.
/// `Channel::close` needs no room, never waits and never gives up, and it is
/// final: anything the lease's fibers send after it is refused, so none of
/// it can reach the next lease, whose channel is a new one.
///
/// The down token goes back into `idle` for the next caller while the pool
/// still has nothing live or reconnecting -- the rule `answer_down` applies,
/// and `tally.token` is where it left its decision. That send cannot wait
/// either: `idle` has room for every slot and one token, and this token is
/// not in it.
fn give_back(idle: Channel<Offer>, tally: Shared<Tally>, offer: Offer) -> () {
  match offer {
    Offer::Slot(requests) => Channel::close(requests),
    Offer::Down => {
      let t = Shared::get(tally);
      if t.token && !t.closing { Channel::send(idle, Offer::Down); () }
    },
  }
}

// --- the serving fiber -------------------------------------------------------

/// One slot, for the pool's whole life: connect, lend, reconnect.
fn slot(
  settings: Settings,
  plan: Reconnect,
  idle: Channel<Offer>,
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
) -> () {
  with { clock: Clock::real(), random: Random::real() } {
    let mut going = true;
    while going {
      match reconnect(settings, plan, idle, tally) {
        Option::None => going = false,
        Option::Some(c) => {
          going = lend(c, idle, tally);
          // **Closed before anything else is tried**, and never read again:
          // whatever the server was still sending on it goes with it.
          hang_up(c);
          if going { moved(tally, idle, Phase::Live, Phase::Reconnecting) };
        },
      }
    };
  }
}

/// Lends `c` until its connection breaks. `false` when the pool is closing.
///
/// **Every lease gets a request channel of its own**, made here and offered
/// in `idle`, and the lease is over when that channel is closed and drained.
/// A fresh channel per lease is what makes the close safe to use as the
/// signal: a fiber from an old lease that still holds its `db` sends to a
/// closed channel and is refused, and nothing it sends can be read as a
/// request of the next lease. The same close is how `close` ends a slot that
/// is waiting in `idle`, so the two are told apart by `tally.closing`, which
/// `close` sets before it closes anything.
fn lend(c: Connection, idle: Channel<Offer>, tally: Shared<Tally>) -> Bool {
  let mut open = true;
  let mut lending = true;
  while lending {
    // Room for one waiting request: a connection answers one at a time, and
    // a lease with more fibers than that makes the rest wait for their turn.
    let requests: Channel<Request> = Channel::bounded(1);
    if !Channel::send(idle, Offer::Slot(requests)) {
      // `idle` is closed: the pool is closing.
      open = false;
      lending = false
    } else {
      serve_lease(c, requests);
      if closing(tally) {
        open = false;
        lending = false
      } else if !usable(c) {
        lending = false
      }
    }
  };
  open
}

/// Connects, on the fast schedule and then the slow interval. `None` when the
/// pool closes first, or when the slot shrinks for good.
fn reconnect(
  settings: Settings,
  plan: Reconnect,
  idle: Channel<Offer>,
  tally: Shared<Tally>,
) -> Option<Connection>
  with { clock: Clock, random: Random }
{
  let began = clock.monotonic_millis();
  let mut attempts = 0;
  let mut phase = Phase::Reconnecting;
  let mut outcome: Option<Connection> = Option::None;
  let mut going = true;
  while going {
    if closing(tally) {
      going = false
    } else {
      match connect(settings, plan.handshake) {
        Result::Ok(c) => {
          if closing(tally) {
            // Opened just as the pool closed: nobody will lend it.
            hang_up(c)
          } else {
            outcome = Option::Some(c)
          };
          going = false
        },
        Result::Err(why) => {
          remember(tally, why);
          attempts = attempts + 1;
          let wait = match phase {
            Phase::Down => slow_wait(plan),
            // Not reached: a slot only reconnects from `Reconnecting` or
            // `Down`. Stopping is the answer that cannot loop.
            Phase::Live => Option::None,
            Phase::Reconnecting => {
              let now = clock.monotonic_millis() - began;
              match Schedule::next_after(plan.fast, attempts, now) {
                Option::Some(at) => Option::Some(at - now),
                Option::None => {
                  phase = Phase::Down;
                  moved(tally, idle, Phase::Reconnecting, Phase::Down);
                  slow_wait(plan)
                },
              }
            },
          };
          match wait {
            Option::None => going = false,
            Option::Some(millis) => if !pause(tally, millis) { going = false },
          }
        },
      }
    }
  };
  match outcome {
    Option::Some(c) => {
      moved(tally, idle, phase, Phase::Live);
      Option::Some(c)
    },
    Option::None => Option::None,
  }
}

/// The slow interval with 80-120% jitter, or `None` for a permanent shrink.
fn slow_wait(plan: Reconnect) -> Option<Int> with { random: Random } {
  match plan.slow {
    Option::None => Option::None,
    Option::Some(every) => Option::Some(every * random.in_range(80, 121) / 100),
  }
}

/// How long one slice of a reconnect's wait is.
///
/// **What this bounds: how long `close` waits for a slot that is between
/// attempts.** The runtime has no receive with a deadline, so a slot cannot
/// sleep on something `close` could wake; it sleeps in slices and looks at
/// the pool between them. The cost is a wake every slice per waiting slot.
fn slice() -> Int { 25 }

/// Waits `millis`, or less if the pool closes. `false` if it closed.
fn pause(tally: Shared<Tally>, millis: Int) -> Bool with { clock: Clock } {
  let until = clock.monotonic_millis() + millis;
  let mut open = true;
  let mut waiting = true;
  while waiting {
    let left = until - clock.monotonic_millis();
    if closing(tally) {
      open = false;
      waiting = false
    } else if left <= 0 {
      waiting = false
    } else {
      clock.sleep(if left < slice() { left } else { slice() })
    }
  };
  open
}

fn closing(tally: Shared<Tally>) -> Bool {
  Shared::get(tally).closing
}

/// Keeps `why` as the reason a pool with nothing live gives its callers.
fn remember(tally: Shared<Tally>, why: DbError) -> () {
  let text = match why {
    DbError::Disconnected(m) => m,
    DbError::Rejected(m) => m,
    DbError::RolledBack(m) => m,
  };
  Shared::update(tally, fn t => {
      live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: text,
      closing: t.closing, token: t.token,
    });
}

/// Moves one slot from `from` to `to`, and puts the down token into `idle`
/// when that leaves nothing live or reconnecting.
fn moved(tally: Shared<Tally>, idle: Channel<Offer>, from: Phase, to: Phase) -> () {
  let token_now = Shared::modify(tally, fn t => {
      let live = t.live + step(Phase::Live, from, to);
      let reconnecting = t.reconnecting + step(Phase::Reconnecting, from, to);
      let down = t.down + step(Phase::Down, from, to);
      let wanted = !t.closing && !t.token && live + reconnecting == 0;
      {
        state: { live: live, reconnecting: reconnecting, down: down, last: t.last,
          closing: t.closing, token: t.token || wanted },
        result: wanted,
      }
    });
  if token_now {
    Channel::send(idle, Offer::Down);
    ()
  };
}

/// What moving a slot from `from` to `to` does to the count of `which`.
fn step(which: Phase, from: Phase, to: Phase) -> Int {
  (if same(which, to) { 1 } else { 0 }) - (if same(which, from) { 1 } else { 0 })
}

fn same(a: Phase, b: Phase) -> Bool {
  match a {
    Phase::Live => match b { Phase::Live => true, Phase::Reconnecting => false, Phase::Down => false },
    Phase::Reconnecting =>
      match b { Phase::Live => false, Phase::Reconnecting => true, Phase::Down => false },
    Phase::Down => match b { Phase::Live => false, Phase::Reconnecting => false, Phase::Down => true },
  }
}

/// Stops every serving fiber and waits for them.
///
/// **Prompt, including for slots that are reconnecting.** The pool is marked
/// closing first; a slot between attempts sees that within one `slice`, a slot
/// that finishes opening a connection after it hangs up at once, and the idle
/// ones are ended by closing their request channels. A connection somebody is
/// using is not closed underneath them: its lease ends when their `with_db`
/// returns, and its serving fiber, finding `idle` closed, hangs up then. So
/// waiting on the crew is what makes this a shutdown rather than a request
/// to shut down.
///
/// What it cannot cut short: a connection attempt already under way. One in
/// the startup exchange ends within the plan's `handshake` bound, when its
/// read gives up; one still inside the operating system's TCP `connect` ends
/// when that does, which for an address that drops packets is the system's
/// connect timeout -- minutes on Linux.
pub fn close(pool: Pool) -> () {
  Shared::update(pool.tally, fn t => {
      live: t.live, reconnecting: t.reconnecting, down: t.down, last: t.last,
      closing: true, token: t.token,
    });
  Channel::close(pool.idle);
  let mut going = true;
  while going {
    match Channel::receive(pool.idle) {
      Option::None => going = false,
      Option::Some(Offer::Slot(requests)) => Channel::close(requests),
      Option::Some(Offer::Down) => (),
    }
  };
  // **The count is discarded here, and only here.** `Fibers::wait` answers
  // how many children ended with an error, and this is a shutdown: the serving
  // fibers were stopped on purpose a few lines above, by closing the channels
  // they were waiting on. A pool that raised on the way out would be reporting
  // its own request to stop as a fault, and there is nobody left to tell.
  let _stopped = Fibers::wait(pool.crew);
}
